    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Africa Twin Gets Biggest Refresh in a Decade, They Now Call It CRF1150L\nKarmenda Karosserie's Air-Cooled Sports Cars Bring Volkswagen Underpinnings and Six-Figure Prices\n7 photos\nPhoto: Karmenda Karosserie\nA six-figure sports car with a reinforced and titled Volkswagen chassis and Porsche 356 styling? That is exactly what Karmenda Karosserie will be producing from the second quarter of 2027, and the Atlantic Beach, Florida-based company has three distinct models to choose from.\nHand-built cars rather than restomods, that is, with said models capturing the appeal of classic German machinery at a fraction of the 356's price. Speaking of which, a highly original 356 can easily change hands for more than $400,000 nowadays. The Karmenda Karosserie trio, meanwhile, is listed with prices ranging from $134,100 to $189,600 before options.\nReferred to as the Kollection, said trio comprises an off-roady coupe, a conventional sports car of the coupe variety, and an open-top speedster. Type S is what Karmenda calls the cheapest and least complicated proposition, which uses a 150-horsepower engine and a shortened chassis.\nThe lightest of the bunch, this fellow targets people who enjoy the mechanical experience rather than customers obsessed with performance figures. Its rear suspension is a swing-axle setup that incorporates a zero-roll system inspired by Formula Vee, namely a low-cost racing class where the open-wheel cars feature stock or lightly modified flat-four mills of the air-cooled variety.\nAt $178,900 from the outset, the Karmenda Karosserie Type C takes the same philosophy in a sporty GT direction. It shares its engine with the Type X, namely a 2.3-liter flattie with roughly 200 horsepower on tap, with the Type C putting an emphasis on sporty driving.\nIt features large cross-drilled disc brakes at all four corners, which should come in handy on winding back roads, and customers have a choice between a tall-geared four or an optional five-speed manual.\nPhoto: Karmenda Karosserie Era-correct dual exhaust tips are on the menu as well, along with 15-inch wheels that are 5-inch wide up front and 7 for the rear axle. Inside, you get fixed-back leather GT seats. All three models are further advertised with a 2-year/20,000-mile transferable warranty.\nThe Type X is, hands down, my favorite. While it may take the familiar 356 silhouette, this model gives it an entirely different look due to 12 inches (305 millimeters) of suspension travel, all-terrain rubber, skid plates, rally lights, and a roof rack holding a full-size spare. The safari-style rack is an $1,200 option.\nStyling-wise, Karmenda Karosserie draws inspiration from Volkswagen 's participation in the East Africa Safari. Its website references the 1953 Coronation Safari, when competitors tackled just around 3,000 miles of largely unpaved roads through Kenya, Uganda, and Tanganyika. The latter united with Zanzibar in 1964, forming the United Republic of Tanzania.\nAlso equipped with an off-road differential, the Type X features widebody fender flares because it gains 6 inches (152 millimeters) of track width and 5 inches (127 millimeters) of wheelbase. Under the Heritage program, the company uses original steel-bodied coupes as the foundation of the X and C, with prices kicking off at $298,100 and $281,800, respectively.\nThe Heritage Type S, meanwhile, comes with a new steel body made to original factory specifications. For a cool $32,400, the company is much obliged to replace the standard four-pot with a Porsche engine.\nCustomers may reserve any of the three models with a $5,000 deposit, followed by 50 percent of the build's final price when production starts. A 10 percent introductory discount is available on every vehicle commissioned by March 7, 2027.\nVia // Karmenda Karosserie\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nKarmenda Karosserie Karmenda Volkswagen Porsche custom\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Fully-Equipped Camper Van Offers Self-Sufficiency, Year-Round Capability, and a Homey Interior",
